<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liechtenstein</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Principality of Liechtenstein, Fürstentum Liechtenstein</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liechtenstein, officially the Principality of Liechtenstein, is a doubly landlocked microstate in the Central European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Its territory was formed on 23 January 1719, when Emperor Charles VI united Vaduz and Schellenberg and raised them to an imperial principality named after Anton Florian of Liechtenstein. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> ended in 1806, and the prince joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and later the German Confederation. The principality became fully independent when that confederation dissolved in 1866. It has been a monarchy headed by a prince since.</p></div>
